    "slug": "forestal-vina-del-mar",
    "file": "/images/zonas/forestal-vina-del-mar.webp",
    "commonsTitle": "File:Conjunción planetaria cerrada entre Venus y Júpiter sobre la Loma Latorre de Santa Julia en Viña del Mar. Close planetary conjunction between Venus and Jupiter above Loma Latorre de Santa Julia, Viña del Mar.jpg",
    "sourceUrl": "https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Conjunci%C3%B3n_planetaria_cerrada_entre_Venus_y_J%C3%BApiter_sobre_la_Loma_Latorre_de_Santa_Julia_en_Vi%C3%B1a_del_Mar._Close_planetary_conjunction_between_Venus_and_Jupiter_above_Loma_Latorre_de_Santa_Julia,_Vi%C3%B1a_del_Mar.jpg",
    "originalUrl": "https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/7/7f/Conjunci%C3%B3n_planetaria_cerrada_entre_Venus_y_J%C3%BApiter_sobre_la_Loma_Latorre_de_Santa_Julia_en_Vi%C3%B1a_del_Mar._Close_planetary_conjunction_between_Venus_and_Jupiter_above_Loma_Latorre_de_Santa_Julia%2C_Vi%C3%B1a_del_Mar.jpg?utm_source=commons.wikimedia.org&utm_campaign=imageinfo&utm_content=original",
    "author": "Kinosmosis",
    "license": "CC BY-SA 4.0",
    "licenseUrl": "https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by-sa/4.0",
    "description": "Close planetary conjunction between Venus and Jupiter above Loma Latorre de Santa Julia, Viña del Mar (August 11, 2025). This close approach was closely followed by the astronomical community, as it brought together two of the brightest celestial objects visible from Earth, only surpassed by the Sun and the Moon: Venus, with a magnitude of –3.9, and the gas giant Jupiter, with a magnitude of –1.8. The phenomenon unfolded between August 11 and 13, with the planets separated by 1° of angular distance on the 11th, and reaching their closest apparent approach of less than 1° on the 12th. In reality, this was only a visual effect: during those dates Venus was about 68 million miles from Earth, while Jupiter was nearly 565 million miles away. The actual distance between both planets was approximately 497 million miles. The submitted photograph was taken on August 11 from Loma Latorre de Santa Julia, in Viña del Mar, under clear sky conditions. It is a vertical panorama composed of three horizontal frames, each captured with a focal length of 400 mm, designed to expand the landscape beneath the sky’s horizon and include more foreground elements. The composition spans approximately 3.7 miles of terrain between the observation point and the water tower located at the top of Loma Latorre Hill, crossing a broad urban sector that includes the neighborhoods of Forestal, Miraflores Alto, Achupallas, and part of Villa Independencia. Viña del Mar, in addition to being a well-known coastal city in central Chile famous for its beaches and seaside resorts, has undergone significant urban growth that has expanded from the flat downtown area into the hillsides and ravines, reaching elevations of up to 1,300 feet (400 m). This process has created a landscape defined by ravines, slopes, and layered urban structures, which in the photograph visually interact with the astronomical phenomenon being documented.",
    "modifications": "Recorte 3:2, redimensionado y optimización WebP para uso web.",
    "importedAt": "2026-08-05T21:03:14.632Z",
    "filename": "forestal-vina-del-mar.webp",
    "landing": "/zona/forestal-vina-del-mar"
  },
